 ],
 "literal": "Having raised a stone heap on the pebbly side, / having adorned it with a chaplet of red flowers strung with split bowstring hemp, / having adorned it with beautiful peacock feathers and engraved his name, / now they indeed planted the stone. / Having brought back the milch cows with their calves and driven away the enemies, / not knowing that the noble hero has passed away, / will the group of bards come even today?",
 "translation": "On the pebbly slope, they raised a mound of stones. They adorned it with a chaplet of red flowers strung on split bowstring hemp, decorated it with beautiful peacock feathers, engraved his name, and have now planted this hero-stone. Unaware that the noble hero—who rescued the milch cows along with their calves and drove away the enemies—has passed away, will the troupe of bards still come today seeking his patronage?",
 "modern": "பருக்கைக் கற்கள் நிறைந்த பக்கத்தில் ஒரு கல் குவியலை எழுப்பி, பிளந்த மரல் நாரால் தொடுக்கப்பட்ட சிவந்த பூக்களாலான மாலையைச் சூட்டி, அழகான மயில்தோகையை அணிவித்து, அவனது பெயரைப் பொறித்து, இப்போது நடுகல்லை நட்டுவிட்டார்கள். கன்றுகளோடு கூடிய பசுக்களை மீட்டுக்கொண்டு வந்து பகைவர்களை விரட்டியடித்த அந்தப் பெருந்தகை இறந்துவிட்டதை அறியாமல், பாணர்களின் சுற்றம் இன்றும் அவனிடம் பரிசு பெற வருவார்களோ?",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Puram genre, specifically the Karanthai tiṇai (cattle recovery) and Kaiyaṟunilai tuṟai (state of helplessness). The poet, Uṟaiyūr Iḷampoṉ Vāṇikaṉār, laments the death of a young hero who died while recovering stolen cattle from enemies. The villagers have raised a hero-stone (naṭukal) on a pebbly slope, decorated it with peacock feathers and red flowers, and engraved his name on it. The poet wonders with deep sorrow whether the traveling bards (pāṇar), unaware of his death, will still come today expecting his usual generous patronage. The key words are 'naṭṭaṉarē kallum' (they planted the stone) and 'neṭuntakai kaḻintamai aṟiyātu' (not knowing that the noble hero has passed away).",
